{0 Forms and file uploads}

Use {!Workero.Request.form_data} to read [multipart/form-data] uploads or
[application/x-www-form-urlencoded] fields. This handler checks for an [avatar]
file and returns its name and size.

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match Request.form_data req with
  | form -> (
      match Form_data.get form "avatar" with
      | Some (File file) ->
          Response.text
            (Printf.sprintf "%s: %d bytes\n" (Form_data.file_name file)
               (Form_data.file_size file))
      | Some (Text _) -> Response.text ~status:`Bad_Request "avatar must be a file\n"
      | None -> Response.text ~status:`Bad_Request "no avatar\n")
  | exception Js_error _ -> Response.text ~status:`Bad_Request "not a form\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [npx wrangler dev] in one terminal. In a second terminal, send a file:

{@sh[
printf 'hello' > avatar.txt
curl -F avatar=@avatar.txt http://localhost:8787/
]}

The response is [avatar.txt: 5 bytes]. A missing [avatar] or a non-file value
gets a 400 response. {!Workero.Request.form_data} raises {!Workero.Js_error}
for an unsupported content type or a malformed form body.

{1 Read fields}

A field is a {!Workero.Form_data.value}: [Text of string] or [File of file].

- {!Workero.Form_data.get} gives the first entry with a name.
- {!Workero.Form_data.get_all} gives all entries with a name.
- {!Workero.Form_data.to_list} gives all entries in the order sent. Duplicate
  names stay.

{1 Read files}

- {!Workero.Form_data.file_name} is the file name that the client sent.
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_type} is the [content-type] of the part, or [""].
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_size} is the size in bytes.
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_bytes} reads the whole file into a [string].
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_stream} gives the file as a {!Workero.Stream.t}.
  Reading it in chunks avoids creating another whole-file OCaml [string].

{1 Multipart memory limit}

The platform parses the entire multipart body before {!Workero.Request.form_data}
returns. {!Workero.Form_data.file_stream} does not avoid that buffering.
{{!page-r2}R2} {!Workero.R2.put} accepts a [string], not a stream. To process a
body in chunks, use {!Workero.Request.body} instead of form parsing. See
{{!page-streams}Streams} and
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/#memory}Workers memory limits}.
